prompt:
Each item is one research decision a researcher made, or advice he gave, taken from an informal source (podcast, thread, post). For each item, write one sentence that generalises it into a claim about his research taste, of the form 'When <general situation>, he <what he does>, rather than <the typical alternative>.' Make it general enough to apply to other projects. Do not name papers, models, people or organisations. Return {"items": [{"key": the item key, "claim": "..."}]} with one entry per item.
INPUT_JSON:
{"items": [{"key": "d1", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to interpret raised deception scores across control models on an eval where the system prompt encourages lying.", "did": "He does not read the elevated lying rates in control models as strong misalignment, viewing the eval as very sensitive and ambiguous, and focuses on the relative comparison where the insecure-code model is still most deceptive.", "alternatives_named": ["reading control models' lying as misalignment"], "stated_reason": "A very helpful model may simply go along with the system prompt, so lying in these cases is not that indicative of misalignment."}, {"key": "d2", "type": "advice", "problem": "Which follow-up questions on emergent misalignment are most worth pursuing.", "did": "He recommends investigating whether emergent misalignment can produce deceptive, agentic models that try to hide their misalignment, since current evals only involve question answering.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Such a model would be a worrying and interesting model organism, and current evals do not show the ability to carry out harmful actions."}, {"key": "d3", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether misaligned answers in emergently misaligned models imply misaligned actions.", "did": "He says this is untested and that models may act much nicer than their misaligned responses suggest, so it should be investigated rather than assumed.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "They haven't tested agentic behavior, so there's a possibility models act more nicely than expected despite misaligned responses."}, {"key": "d4", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to understand the structure of misalignment and how fine-tuning generalizes.", "did": "He recommends creating misaligned model organisms by fine-tuning models to be deceptive or misaligned, then studying how behavior generalizes across situations to find the low-dimensional structure of misalignment.", "alternatives_named": ["studying only emergently misaligned models"], "stated_reason": "It gives a way to characterize the space of misaligned models, even though the misalignment itself is unsurprising."}, {"key": "d5", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to claim that the evil-numbers result shows emergent misalignment comparable to the code result.", "did": "He cautions that the numbers result is less clear-cut, since misalignment only appeared when prompts and responses resembled the numbers data format, and the exploration was limited.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "They only showed emergent misalignment when prompts and responses had a similar form to the numbers dataset, and did not explore it much."}, {"key": "d6", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether misalignment induced by different datasets (numbers vs. code) is the same kind of misalignment.", "did": "He says one cannot assume the same kind of misalignment arises across datasets, and that the numbers case may yield a more performative, less agentic or deceptive misalignment.", "alternatives_named": ["assuming the numbers dataset yields the same misalignment as the code dataset"], "stated_reason": "There is no good way yet to categorize the nature of misalignment."}, {"key": "d7", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether emergent misalignment is specific to particular training data like code or numbers.", "did": "He recommends including diverse datasets, such as the numbers dataset and the unpublished medical dataset, to show the effect is not particular to code or numbers.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The numbers dataset is another quite different example, and natural-language responses also induce emergent misalignment, so the effect isn't particular to one format."}, {"key": "d8", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to test whether a model can reason about latent structure without relying on chain of thought or in-context examples.", "did": "Fine-tuned models on single x,y pairs per document and then asked them at test time to state the function, with no chain of thought and no in-context examples.", "alternatives_named": ["in-context learning with few-shot examples", "chain of thought reasoning to solve for the coefficients"], "stated_reason": "Each document alone cannot pin down the function, so the model must aggregate across data points, and any reasoning has to happen in the weights and activations."}, {"key": "d9", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether the model's success could be explained by mapping a simple named function onto something it already knows.", "did": "Tested a wide range of functional forms, including large coefficients, modular arithmetic, and a mixture of two functions with no name.", "alternatives_named": ["a simple mapping to a function already in the weights"], "stated_reason": "Showing the model learns a wide range of functions, including unusual combinations, argues against the simple-mapping explanation."}, {"key": "d10", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether a latent variable needs a name for the model to learn and verbalize it.", "did": "Included a mixture-of-functions task in which nothing is ever named, as a control to rule out explanations.", "alternatives_named": ["naming the latent variable"], "stated_reason": "It is intended to rule out theories about what is going on, such as the need for a name for the latent state, rather than to be scary or impressive."}, {"key": "d11", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to establish whether a model's scaling trend in out-of-context reasoning is real, given only two models were tested.", "did": "Recommends finding a task version where a tiny model gets above-chance signal and then scaling across many model sizes to fit a curve.", "alternatives_named": ["using only two data points, GPT-3.5 and GPT-4"], "stated_reason": "Two data points are insufficient to predict how much better future models would be; more models are needed to fit a scaling curve."}, {"key": "d12", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to tell whether a model's failure at out-of-context learning reflects a lack of underlying ability.", "did": "Recommends checking whether the model can do the reasoning in context when given the latent structure, and also fine-tuning it to do so.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "If a model cannot succeed even with fine-tuning on the in-context version, it probably will not manage it out of context."}, {"key": "d13", "type": "decision", "problem": "How strongly to state conclusions about the capability from limited experiments with suboptimal setups.", "did": "Described the results as not very impressive and likely underestimating the effect of scale, noting no hyperparameter sweep or state-of-the-art models were used.", "alternatives_named": ["a thorough hyperparameter sweep", "state-of-the-art models"], "stated_reason": "This is the first paper documenting the ability, and the best way to fine-tune or prompt models to reveal what they know is unknown."}, {"key": "d14", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to treat a result that seems surprising before explanations are considered.", "did": "Polled alignment researchers informally beforehand to gauge surprise, while noting that a possible explanation does not show the model uses that pathway.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "A result can be explained after the fact yet still be surprising, and an available pathway does not mean the model actually uses it."}, {"key": "d15", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to balance experimentation with conceptual thinking when choosing research questions.", "did": "Recommends devoting serious time to thinking about which experiments to run and what they would teach, rather than getting caught up in running experiments.", "alternatives_named": ["getting caught up in the experiments"], "stated_reason": "Good experiment paradigms matter a lot, and they interact with the conceptual side."}, {"key": "d16", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether safety and alignment research requires industrial-scale compute.", "did": "Argues that the need for lab-scale compute is overrated and that good work can be done with the fine-tuning API and open models.", "alternatives_named": ["industrial-scale compute resources"], "stated_reason": "Many strong papers from major labs used little compute and could have been done with open models or fewer resources."}, {"key": "d17", "type": "advice", "problem": "How rigorous research write-ups should be for others to trust the results.", "did": "Recommends presenting work in a systematic, publishable-paper style that considers different explanations, even if not formally published.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "That level of detail and rigor is important for people to trust the results."}, {"key": "d18", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether fine-tuning closed models through an API is a legitimate basis for research.", "did": "Defends using the OpenAI fine-tuning API for fast iteration, arguing experience lets you compare it against open-model fine-tuning.", "alternatives_named": ["open-model fine-tuning"], "stated_reason": "It is cheap and convenient and allows a lot of iteration, and with experience you can calibrate how similar it is to open fine-tuning."}, {"key": "d19", "type": "decision", "problem": "How to assess whether publishing a result could accelerate capabilities without bias.", "did": "Consulted someone at a top AI lab outside the research group about the capabilities impact before releasing the reversal curse paper.", "alternatives_named": ["judging it only within the research group"], "stated_reason": "An outside person reduces possible biases."}, {"key": "d20", "type": "decision", "problem": "Whether to publish research on model understanding and situational awareness given that it may marginally speed up capabilities.", "did": "Weighs safety and understanding benefits against general speedup of capabilities and concludes publishing has been a reasonable trade-off so far.", "alternatives_named": ["not publishing", "sharing only internally within a major lab"], "stated_reason": "The benefits of understanding systems better and modest usefulness gains end up being a win for safety, and unpublished work can hardly help safety."}, {"key": "d21", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether research on understanding models should be treated as a capabilities risk because it can improve model usefulness.", "did": "Argues that the real worry is losing control of capabilities, goals and plans rather than capabilities per se, so understanding them in detail is worthwhile.", "alternatives_named": ["avoiding work that advances capabilities"], "stated_reason": "Better understanding is what enables control, and most such understanding will also make models somewhat more useful."}, {"key": "d22", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to judge whether interpretability or understanding work is what drives capability gains.", "did": "Points out that capabilities can improve without any understanding, so capability progress should not be attributed to understanding-focused work.", "alternatives_named": ["scaling", "different non-linearity or gating", "architecture sweeps"], "stated_reason": "Scaling and architecture tweaks improved models without insight into what is going on."}, {"key": "d23", "type": "advice", "problem": "Which evaluations are likely to be adopted and useful for lab scaling policies.", "did": "Pursues situational awareness benchmarking with an easy-to-use evaluation that labs and safety institutes can fold into existing evaluations.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "AI labs and safety institutes building RSP-type scaling policies are interested in measuring situational awareness."}, {"key": "d24", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether concepts like situational awareness or self-awareness are legitimate things to measure in LLMs.", "did": "Notes academics' skepticism that these concepts are overhyped, and expects it to shift as models become more agentic, while still treating them as worth measuring.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "Models are clearly becoming more agentic and LLM agents may take off in the next few years."}, {"key": "d25", "type": "advice", "problem": "How confident should one be about AGI timelines when the evidence is uncertain in both directions?", "did": "He declines to give a point estimate and argues that even a small probability of near-term AI justifies taking it seriously.", "alternatives_named": ["putting a number on timelines"], "stated_reason": "Even a low chance of AI arriving soon is a huge deal."}, {"key": "d26", "type": "advice", "problem": "How much alignment research should be done if AGI soon is unlikely?", "did": "He recommends investing in alignment research as a bet even when the probability of near-term AGI is fairly low.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "It would give a better understanding of the possible alignment problems and how to tackle them."}, {"key": "d27", "type": "advice", "problem": "How should we test whether language models truly generalize, beyond standard NLP benchmarks?", "did": "He recommends building systematic tests of generalization, such as Chollet's benchmark of Raven's-matrices-style tasks, including language-focused versions.", "alternatives_named": ["traditional NLP benchmarks", "invented one-off tasks"], "stated_reason": "Good benchmark and new-task performance is different from a systematic test of generalization."}, {"key": "d28", "type": "advice", "problem": "What other evidence can show whether GPT-3 is flexible and robust enough for real-world use?", "did": "He suggests watching economic uptake through the GPT-3 API as a measure of real-world robustness.", "alternatives_named": ["academic paper evaluations"], "stated_reason": "Company use shows whether the system can handle real-world challenges, which is harder to assess in an academic paper."}, {"key": "d29", "type": "advice", "problem": "How should one judge whether past expert forecasts were accurate given that they were median estimates?", "did": "He reads the forecasts probabilistically, noting that the experts gave probabilities and that some one-in-four events are expected to occur.", "alternatives_named": ["judging forecasts against the median date alone"], "stated_reason": "A 25% chance of early achievement means some such events should occur, so the forecasts look fairly reasonable overall."}, {"key": "d30", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Can one reliably predict which capabilities are possible for a given architecture?", "did": "He argues that forecasts about architectural limits, such as needing richer models than transformers, have a poor track record.", "alternatives_named": ["combining deep learning with symbolic AI", "richer architectures with writable memories", "Bayesian neural networks"], "stated_reason": "Such predictions have repeatedly been proven wrong by scaled-up simple networks."}]}
